Because $P$ is equidistant from each vertex of the octahedron, then $P$ lies on the median of each face.  In any of our vertices $P$ lies on the side of the face that is perpendicular to this median.  By Pythagorean theorem, we have \begin{align*}
11^2 + 10^2 &= (3^2 + 7^2) + (8^2 + 9^2) \\
&= (18 + 88) + (64 + 81) \\
&= 100 + 145 \\
&= \boxed{245},
\end{align*} or $2\sqrt{122.5} = 2 \cdot 11 \cdot \sqrt{11.25} = \boxed{44 \cdot \sqrt{11.25}}$
This value makes sense geometrically, because it is the length of the median of the face, which is $\frac{1}{2}$ of the hypotenuse of the right triangle whose legs are 3 and 10.

